[{:seon.task/name "a-private-wait-wakes-for-the-fiber-dump"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-26: kill -INFO on a hung Seon test runner (pid 71239) printed no fiber dump. Its main thread was in a plain thread's read, which jolt.io-poller blocks in a private kqueue of its own (wait-ready on a plain thread: a private kevent); that kqueue has no SIGINFO filter, so the dump's Chez handler never got an event check. The dump (jolt.host/fiber-dump, Jolt d598fc04, docs/forks.md) wakes only a main carrier idle in the poller's own kqueue, which carries EVFILT_SIGNAL for SIGINFO; a test runner, the commonest hang, waits in the private one. Correct, at the cause: the private per-wait kqueue also takes SIGINFO (EVFILT_SIGNAL), so the wait wakes, its thread runs the handler at its event check, and the wait resumes (a signal is no readiness: the loop goes back into kevent). Proof red first: a plain thread blocked reading a pipe nobody writes, kill -INFO to its own process, and the dump's text on stderr while the read still waits (a Jolt unit or smoke case)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}]
